==Battleground==
*Infinite [[Doge]]s spawn, delay 6.67~8 seconds<sup>200f~240f</sup>.
*Infinite [[Snache]]s spawn, delay 13.33~16.67 seconds<sup>400f~500f</sup>.
*When the base reaches 99% HP:
**1 [[Crazed Cat (Enemy)|Crazed Cat]] spawns as the boss.
**Infinite [[Le'boin]]s spawn, delay 33.33~46.67 seconds<sup>1,000f~1,400f</sup>.
**Infinite [[Teacher Bear]]s spawn, delay 60~66.67 seconds<sup>1,800f~2,000f</sup>.
**Infinite [[Croco]]s spawn, delay 6.67~10 seconds<sup>200f~300f</sup>.

==Strategy==
This level is extremely tough without the right strategy, but there are a few things you can do to heavily reduce the difficulty. Obtaining all of the [[Treasure]]s from [[Empire of Cats]] will help massively, especially the attack and defense boosts, [[Worker Cat]] boosts and recharge time decreases. Upgrading the corresponding abilities in the [[Upgrade Menu]] to 20+10 will also help a lot. Using a [[Battle Items#Rich Cat|Rich Cat]] can help by minimizing the need to stall at the beginning of the level for Worker Cat levels, and a [[Battle Items#Sniper the Cat|Sniper the Cat]] can keep Crazed Cat at bay, though if you aren't careful, it may cause Bahamut Cat to miss. A [[Battle Items#Cat CPU|Cat CPU]] can make it so that you don't have to spam your meatshields manually, but it may drain your money depending on your lineup, especially if using [[Salon Cat (Rare Cat)|Paris Cat]].

The recommended way to beat this stage is to stack Bahamut Cats on the beginning peons. A lineup of 5 meatshields including Wall Cat, Dragon Cat and Bahamut is recommended, and additional slots can be used for more attackers. Spam nothing except Wall Cat and Bahamut Cat until you get very close to the enemy base, and use all the extra cash to upgrade the Worker Cat. When near the enemy base, begin spamming the rest of your units as well, especially your meatshields. From here on out, you should constantly be spamming meatshields to impede Crazed Cat's progress. Other units such as Magica, Dragon, Bahamut and other attackers should only be sent out when you have the cash available because surviving is more a priority than dealing damage on an endurance stage like this.

==='''Recommended Units'''===
*75¢ [[:Category:Meatshield-class Cats|Meatshields]] (i.e. [[Cat (Normal Cat)|Cat]], [[Boogie Cat (Special Cat)|Boogie Cat]], [[Catburger (Special Cat)|Catburger]], [[Li'l Cat (Special Cat)|Li'l Cat]], etc.): Meatshields are vital for slowing down Crazed Cat. With the exception of Eraser Cat, most meatshields can't survive an attack from Crazed Cat, so cheaper is better. Using around 3~4 is recommended, with any more potentially taking up too many slots and wasting money, while any fewer can cause Crazed Cat to push forward easily.
*[[Tank Cat (Normal Cat)|Eraser Cat]]: Wall Cat's [[True Form]] grants it just enough health to survive one hit from Crazed Cat. If you don't have Eraser Cat and aren't using Magica Cat to weaken Crazed Cat, just replace it with another 75¢ meatshield.
*[[Onmyoji Cat (Rare Cat)|Magica Cat]]: Capable of weakening Crazed Cat from somewhat long range, which can help other units, such as Wall Cat or Dragon Cat, survive more hits. They are outranged by Teacher Bear and Le'boin, so watch out for that.
*[[Mer-Cat (Rare Cat)|Cameraman Cat]]: While you'd likely have to go out of your way to have her by now, Cameraman Cat is a great attacker here. She outranges Crazed Cat, is spammable, and has enough health to survive an attack from Crazed Cat, even without activating her Survive ability. She needs her True Form to provide any value though.
*[[Driller Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Driller Cat]]: While it's not too likely you have them yet, if you do, they can be an excellent crowd-controller here, able to weaken Crazed Cat by a huge amount. Their weaken doesn't have full uptime, but that can be fixed by stacking them on peons at the beginning of the stage. They have even higher range than Bahamut Cat, but their Single Attack means they might waste an attack on a peon.
*[[Lizard Cat (Normal Cat)|Dragon Cat]]: Has relatively long range, and can be easily stacked to deal great damage to Crazed Cat. Unlike Bahamut Cat, they don't outrange Le'boin, so be wary of that.
*[[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Bahamut Cat]]: Long range, high health and higher damage make him an excellent attacker for this stage. He also has enough damage to kill the Teacher Bears and Le'boins in one hit.
*[[Ururun Wolf (Special Cat)|Ururun Wolf]]: While it's generally advised to complete this stage before getting Ururun, in the case you don't, Ururun provides a great mix of both attack and defense. Her knockback can push Crazed Cat back behind the Teacher Bears and Le'boins, allowing your other units to land hits and kill them, but her knockback can also make Bahamut Cat miss an attack.
*[[Adult Cat (Rare Cat)|Prisoner Cat]]: If you get Prisoner, this stage is almost free. Stack them up with the beginning Doges and pair him up with 5 meatshields to hold off Crazed Cat. He would snipe the backliners and the base before Crazed Cat can do anything.

==='''Recommended Ubers'''===
*Decent Mid-Backline range Ubers: Take what you can for this stage; as long as the Uber can outrange Crazed Cat and the backliners safely and deal good damage, you may consider bringing them if they're well-levelled.
*[[EPICFEST (Gacha Event)|EPICFEST]] exclusives: This entire stage is composed of Traitless enemies, making it straight up food for units such as [[Shadow Gao (Uber Rare Cat)|Shadow Gao]].
*[[Relentless Gladios (Uber Rare Cat)|Relentless Gladios]]: None of the enemies have enough range that Gladios should be concerned. Gladios itself can stunlock Crazed Cat at the base, alleviating a lot of pressure.
*[[Ninja Girl Tomoe (Uber Rare Cat)|Ninja Girl Tomoe]]: She has a decent cooldown, a decent chance to freeze traitless, and her LD allows her to potentially snipe the base and end the fight early.

